# Tooling and quality gates

Tooling exists to provide fast, repeatable evidence that the project satisfies
its engineering standards.

Security-specific tooling and supply-chain policy are defined in
[devsecops.md](devsecops.md). Testing policy and coverage requirements are
defined in [testing.md](testing.md).

## Tool selection

- Prefer established tools from the selected ecosystem.
- Use the language or platform’s native package manager and configuration.
- Use one primary tool for each responsibility.
- Add a tool only when it enforces a real requirement or detects a credible
  defect not covered adequately elsewhere.
- Avoid overlapping tools that produce substantially the same evidence.
- Prefer a maintained dependency over custom automation when automation is
  genuinely required.
- Do not create custom linters, formatters, type checkers, test runners,
  coverage parsers, dependency scanners or build systems.
- Do not add a task runner merely to hide a single native command.
- Ecosystem-standard package scripts are acceptable.
- Do not introduce a cross-language task runner by default.
- Remove a tool when its signal no longer justifies its maintenance cost.

## Complexity and size

- Enforce the profile’s approved cyclomatic-complexity limit of 10 with the
  existing primary linter when supported.
- Treat a function over 50 logical lines as a review signal.
- Treat a source file over 400 logical lines as a responsibility-review signal.
- Treat function and file size as review signals rather than universal CI
  failures.
- Do not add Radon or another metrics tool solely to count lines or produce a
  maintainability score.
- Scripts do not receive a quality exemption simply because they are scripts.

Hard line-count gates are prohibited because they encourage pointless
splitting and metric gaming.

## Toolchain versions

- Declare supported language and runtime versions explicitly.
- Pin toolchain versions using the selected ecosystem’s native mechanism.
- Pin package-manager versions when the ecosystem supports it.
- Keep version constraints in native configuration rather than duplicating them
  in documentation.
- Upgrade toolchains intentionally and verify the complete project afterward.
- Do not rely on an undeclared globally installed version.

## Environment and dependencies

- Use only the selected ecosystem package manager for dependency operations.
- Keep the project-local development environment outside version control.
- Commit the native lockfile.
- Never edit a generated lockfile manually.
- Add and remove dependencies through the package manager.
- Separate runtime, development and optional dependencies using native groups.
- Declare every direct dependency explicitly.
- Do not promote transitive dependencies into direct dependencies without an
  actual import or runtime requirement.
- Remove dependencies made unused by the current change.
- Prefer the standard library and existing dependencies for small requirements.
- Review dependency security and supply-chain impact according to
  [devsecops.md](devsecops.md).

## Configuration ownership

- Keep each tool’s configuration in its native conventional location.
- Use `pyproject.toml` or an equivalent ecosystem file when the tool supports
  it well.
- Define each threshold and exclusion once in enforceable configuration.
- Keep local and CI commands short by relying on committed configuration.
- Do not duplicate long option lists across documentation and workflows.
- Keep suppressions narrow, local and explained.
- Never disable an entire rule category to suppress one justified exception.
- Update configuration, documentation and CI together when a required gate
  changes.

## Selected toolchain

[[ profile_values.TOOLCHAIN ]]

Every selected tool must be installed or provisioned by the documented setup.
A generated verification command must never depend on an undeclared tool.

## Quality gates

[[ profile_values.QUALITY_GATES ]]

Every applicable gate is mandatory.

- Run gates in strict non-interactive mode.
- Treat warnings as failures when the selected tool supports that policy
  reliably.
- Do not skip, weaken or disable a gate merely to obtain a passing build.
- Do not add meaningless code or tests to satisfy a metric.
- Resolve a valid finding at its cause.
- Document any approved exception at the narrowest supported location.
- Never report a gate as passing unless its command completed successfully.

## Local and CI parity

- Local development and CI use the same committed configuration.
- CI runs the same required project checks documented below.
- CI may add platform, release or credential-dependent checks that cannot run
  locally.
- Commands must be deterministic and non-interactive.
- Use check mode in CI; CI must not modify or reformat the working tree.
- Test supported operating systems only when the Vision requires them.
- Do not create a platform matrix for hypothetical portability.
- Keep required checks independent of a developer’s global machine state.

## Feedback speed

Provide two documented verification paths:

1. **Fast feedback** for the checks appropriate during development.
2. **Required verification** for every gate needed before completion.

Both paths use native commands. The fast path is not sufficient for completion.

- Keep fast checks focused and deterministic.
- Run independent CI checks in parallel when it materially reduces feedback
  time.
- Cache only reproducible package-manager and tool data.
- Never cache secrets, mutable test state or build outputs whose provenance is
  uncertain.
- Measure CI duration before adding caching or orchestration complexity.

## Local hooks

- Hooks are optional developer feedback, not the only enforcement point.
- Use an established hook manager when hooks are selected.
- Keep pre-commit hooks fast.
- Use the configured `commit-msg` hook to enforce Conventional Commits.
- Keep expensive or platform-dependent checks in required verification and CI.
- Do not install or modify global hooks without explicit user approval.
- CI remains responsible for enforcing every mandatory gate.

## Generated artifacts

- Generate artifacts deterministically.
- Identify generated files clearly.
- Do not edit generated files directly.
- Regenerate from the owning source and review the resulting diff.
- When generated artifacts are committed, CI must detect stale output using the
  owning standard tool.

## Dependency and release automation

- Dependabot proposes weekly updates for GitHub Actions and the selected
  ecosystem.
- Release Please derives Semantic Versioning releases and changelog entries
  from Conventional Commits.
- Release Please owns release version updates. Do not edit version files in a
  release pull request unless the automation configuration is being repaired.

## Commands

Run commands from the repository root.

### Environment setup

```text
[[ profile_values.SETUP_COMMANDS ]]
```

### Fast feedback

```text
[[ profile_values.FAST_CHECK_COMMANDS ]]
```

### Required verification

```text
[[ profile_values.VERIFY_COMMANDS ]]
```

If a required command cannot run, report the exact command and reason. Never
claim successful verification for a command that was not executed.
